8. + adds permissions, - removes them, = replaces selected permissions
9. Each operation uses the mode resulting from preceding operations
10. Permission copying copies source class's ordinary rwx bits to selected classes
11. Conditional X supplies search permission for directories
12. Empty assignments clear selected ordinary permissions and are valid even at the end of an expression
13. With explicit who (including a), ordinary symbolic permission changes are independent of umask
14. With omitted who, + and - affect only ordinary permission bits not protected by process umask; = replaces ordinary permissions with requested permissions filtered by that umask, clearing other ordinary permissions
15. Preserve GNU special-bit semantics including inherited directory set-user-ID/set-group-ID bits
16. Numeric modes of 4 or fewer octal digits preserve unspecified directory set-ID bits; 5+ digits explicitly clear them
17. s addresses set-user-ID for u and set-group-ID for g; t addresses sticky for o (including a or omitted who)
18. Ordinary assignments preserve directory set-user-ID and set-group-ID bits unless explicitly addressed; sticky clearing follows the selected assignment
19. `+t` and `a+t` have same result with ordinary permissions 0755 for all three umasks
20. `a=rwx,+t` retains explicitly assigned ordinary permissions, equivalent to octal 1777
21. With parents enabled, explicit modes apply only to newly created final directories
22. Newly created intermediate parents retain the parents-checkpoint umask rule with owner write and search ensured
23. Existing directories retain all permissions; -m does not chmod existing final directories accepted by -p

